const onePager=`# TaxProof — core idea and data model\n\nTaxProof turns the workpaper into an evidence-led review workspace. A return number connects to calculation inputs, exact source fields, verification records, decisions and approvals. Original source content is never replaced with extracted output.\n\n## Data model\nThe immutable supplied packet holds documents/pages, extracted values, three return versions, rules, prior-year data, client evidence and timeline events. Stable reference IDs connect these records. The mutable workpaper holds adjustments, verification records, issues, decisions, document dispositions, tasks, questions, attachments, time entries and sign-offs. Every action adds an audit event. A content revision identifies what was reviewed. An optimistic database revision prevents silent concurrent overwrites. Filing snapshots preserve the reviewed state; amendments create a new branch without overwriting snapshots.\n\n## Implementation\nReact/TypeScript interface with browser IndexedDB persistence and attachment storage. All supplied source data is fictional and publicly downloadable. The role switcher intentionally simulates firm staff inside a browser-only demo session. Browser-side role checks demonstrate action eligibility. They are not a security boundary. Sensitive views are masked by default for demonstration; all fictional source data remains available in downloaded code. Unmasked views are logged locally.\n\n## Tradeoffs\nAll substantive changes conservatively stale all approvals rather than selectively invalidating only dependent areas. Tax effects use packet marginal rates and remain estimates; engine lines are not silently recomputed. Precomputed scenarios remain independent. Filing, agency responses and signatures are simulations. No runtime LLM is used; deterministic checks are reproducible. AI assisted coding and documentation.\n\n## Remaining production work\nBind staff roles to real identities; implement validated e-signature and tax software integrations; targeted approval invalidation; scheduled retention and external notifications; broader tax-rule and extraction validation; formal security and accessibility review.\n`;
const findings=`# TaxProof — initial findings\n\nThese findings are not an exhaustive tax audit.\n\n1. Likely duplicated Northlake sweep interest: D28/ev-0492 and D29/ev-0538 describe $1,840 for the same account ending 7731. v3 includes separate Schedule B lines. The independent check exposes both source records. Review confirmation and correction are recorded separately.\n2. Missing Meridian transaction: D31 summary $71,965 vs extracted detail $71,715 leaves $250. Page 32 contains lot M18 with $250 proceeds and $310 basis. The review opens that exact unextracted page. The gain/loss impact is not the same as missing proceeds.\n3. Ambiguous donation: D64/ev-1049 prints $?,500.00; extraction chose $1,500 at 0.41 confidence. This is unresolved evidence, not a confirmed replacement amount.\n4. Duplicate W-2 correctly excluded: D09/D10 are the same W-2. The current wage line already counts it once. It should be documented, not reported as a current double-counting error.\n5. California wage conflict for review: D09 Box 16 $111,300 vs D73 California taxable wages $22,469 through the work-location change. Requires documented allocation review; automatic substitution is inappropriate.\n\nThe app also imports supplied engine diagnostics and independently surfaces material prior-year Form 1040 movements. Confidence does not equal verification. Resolving an issue never changes numbers silently.\n`;
const videoScript=`# TaxProof walkthrough — record yourself, approximately 6–8 minutes\n\n0:00 Explain the idea: tax software computes; TaxProof makes the evidence and review visible.\n0:45 Show overview, balances, document accounting and open items.\n1:30 Open duplicate-interest issue. Inspect D28 and D29, their exact fields and affected return lines.\n2:30 Open a Schedule B line and record an adjustment with evidence. Explain estimated impact versus engine recomputation.\n3:30 Resolve the issue with a documented conclusion. Switch to reviewer, record approval, then make a new substantive change and demonstrate stale approval.\n4:30 Show missing brokerage transaction on D31 page 32. Explain why missing proceeds and taxable gain differ.\n5:15 Show version comparison and one supplied what-if.\n6:00 Show filing blockers, frozen-record workflow, attachments and export.\n7:00 Explain React, browser IndexedDB storage, same-browser revision checks, original fictional packet, role-demo limitations and AI assistance.\n\nRecord your actual screen and voice. Upload the video yourself and provide its link with the app, one-page concept and findings. This script is not a completed video.\n`;
